### 1. Register a New User
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "user@army.mil",
  "password": "SecurePassword123!"
}

###

### 2. Login and Catch the Token
# This request logs in, and the script below it catches the JWT and saves it as {{auth_token}}
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "user@army.mil",
  "password": "SecurePassword123!"
}

> {%
    // This tells IntelliJ: Look at the response body.
    // Save it as a global variable named 'auth_token' so we can use it later
    // If your backend returns JSON like {"token": "ey..."}, change this to response.body.token)
    client.global.set("auth_token", response.body);
    client.test("Request executed successfully", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "Response status is not 200");
    });
%}

###

### 3. Test a Protected Route (The Checkpoint)
GET http://localhost:8080/api/quizzes/[QUIZ_ID_HERE]
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}

### 4. Create a New Quiz (The Full POST Test)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/quizzes
Authorization: Bearer {{auth_token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "My First Full Database Quiz",
  "questions": [
    {
      "questionNumber": 1,
      "text": "What is the capital of Texas?",
      "options": ["Austin", "Dallas", "Houston", "San Antonio"],
      "correctAnswers": ["Austin"]
    },
    {
      "questionNumber": 2,
      "text": "Which of these are programming languages?",
      "options": ["Java", "HTML", "Python", "CSS"],
      "correctAnswers": ["Java", "Python"]
    }
  ]
}